kets' tool_call_id='call_06_9vDD4bzQmIpDXU8yYeO36657'\n\nDeterministic checks already computed against the report:\n- instrument_identity: FAIL (report references company 'Apple Inc.')\n- analysis_date: PASS (report references analysis date 2026-07-31)\n- final_decision_present: PASS (final trade decision is present in the final state)\n- frozen_price_used: PASS (frozen latest close 308.91 present in evidence/corpus)\n- source_id_referenced: FAIL (report references source_id 'src-aapl')\n\nJudge the report below. Score support and relevance on 0-1 using the rubric:\n- support 1.0 = every key conclusion has direct support from the evidence; 0.8 = main conclusions supported with no key fabrication; 0.5 = important evidence gaps; 0 = key factual fabrication.\n- relevance 1.0 = fully focused on the requested ticker/date/task; 0.8 = core complete with minor redundancy; 0.5 = missing key parts; 0 = off task.\n\nCitation accounting (count factual claims that carry a source citation):\n- cited_claim_count = number of factual claims that cite a source ID.\n- supported_cited_claim_count = number of those cited claims whose cited source ID is present AND actually supports that particular claim. A source ID merely existing is NOT support; it must back the specific claim.\n- invalid_citation_explanations = for each cited claim whose citation does not support it, explain why.\n- uncited_factual_claims = factual claims (numbers/dates/identity/price) made with no supporting source ID.\nDo NOT give 1.0 just because the report is nonempty.\n\nREPORT:\nI now have all the data needed. Let me compile the analysis.\n\n**Analysis of AAPL as of 2026-07-31**\n\n**Price Action:** AAPL closed at **$308.91** on 2026-07-31, down sharply from $338.19 on 07-29 and $340.08 on 07-28. The stock hit a day low of $300.00 and high of $310.69 on massive volume (132.5M shares \u2014 the highest in the dataset, ~2.5x normal). This is a significant single-day breakdown after a strong rally from ~$275 (June 25) to a peak of ~$344.57 (July 29 intraday).\n\n**Trend Context:** From May lows near $276, AAPL rallied steadily to $340+ by late July \u2014 a ~23% advance. The July 31 drop of ~$29 (-8.6% from prior close) is a major reversal signal, breaking below the 10 EMA ($327.24) and Bollinger middle ($324.37).\n\n**Indicator Readings:**\n- **10 EMA:** $327.24 \u2014 price ($308.91) is well below, confirming short-term bearish momentum.\n- **Bollinger:** Middle $324.37, Upper $344.51, Lower $304.22. Price closed just above the lower band ($308.91 vs $304.22), suggesting oversold conditions short-term.\n- **RSI:** 45.08 \u2014 neutral, not yet oversold, leaving room for further downside.\n- **MACD:** 6.82 vs signal 8.14, histogram -1.32 \u2014 bearish crossover forming; momentum rolling over.\n- **ATR:** $9.90 \u2014 elevated volatility, consistent with the large daily range.\n- **VWMA:** $308.54 \u2014 price ($308.91) is essentially at the volume-weighted average, indicating the selloff brought price back to the average cost of recent trading.\n\n**Key Levels:** Support at $300 (today's low) and the Bollinger lower band ($304.22). Resistance at $324-327 (Bollinger middle / 10 EMA) and $333-340 (recent highs).\n\n**Actionable Insight:** The sharp reversal on record volume after a parabolic run suggests distribution. RSI at 45 leaves downside room. Watch $300 support \u2014 a break could accelerate selling toward the June consolidation zone ($290-295). The MACD bearish crossover supports a cautious stance.\n\nFINAL TRANSACTION PROPOSAL: **HOLD**\n\n| Metric | Value | Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Last Close | $308.91 | -8.6% single-day drop |\n| Day Range | $300.00\u2013$310.69 | Wide, high volatility |\n| Volume | 132.5M (record) | Distribution risk |\n| 10 EMA | $327.24 | Price below \u2192 bearish |\n| Bollinger Mid/UB/LB | $324.37/$344.51/$304.22 | Near lower band |\n| RSI | 45.08 | Neutral, room to fall |\n| MACD / Signal / Hist | 6.82 / 8.14 / -1.32 | Bearish crossover |\n| ATR | $9.90 | Elevated volatility |\n| VWMA | $308.54 | Price at avg cost |\n| Support | $300 / $304 | Critical zone |\n| Resistance | $324\u2013327 / $333\u2013340 | Rebound targets |\n\nBased on the available data, I'll compile the report. Note that global news, macro indicators, and prediction markets returned no data in this frozen evaluation corpus, so I'll rely on the company-specific news and clearly flag the unavailable sources.\n\n---\n\n# AAPL News & Macro Research Report \u2014 2026-07-31\n\n## Company-Specific News (AAPL)\n\n**Apple Fiscal Q3 2026 Earnings (reported 2026-07-30, quarter ended June 27):**\n- **Revenue: $109.4B, +16% YoY** \u2014 a strong acceleration, indicating robust iPhone/services demand and favorable FX.\n- **Gross margin: 50.1%**, which included ~2 percentage points of **tariff-refund benefit** \u2014 a one-time tailwind that flatters reported margins.\n- **Diluted EPS: $2.02, +29% YoY**, including $0.11 of tariff-refund benefit.\n- **Dividend: $0.27/share** maintained.\n\n**Key interpretation for traders:**\n- The 16% revenue growth is well above Apple's typical mid-single-digit trajectory, signaling a strong product cycle and resilient consumer spending.\n- The tariff-refund benefit is **non-recurring**; strip it out and underlying gross margin is ~48%, still healthy but less spectacular. EPS ex-benefit is ~$1.91.\n- Watch for guidance commentary on whether tariff relief persists into Q4/FY2027 \u2014 this is the swing factor for forward estimates.\n\n## Macro & Global Context\n- **Global news, macro indicators (CPI, Fed funds, 10Y Treasury), and prediction markets returned NO DATA** in this frozen evaluation corpus. These sources are unavailable; no values were estimated or fabricated.\n- **Actionable caveat:** Without live macro data, rate-cut expectations and recession probabilities cannot be independently verified. Traders should treat the earnings beat as the primary catalyst and monitor Fed policy and inflation prints separately.\n\n## Trading Implications for AAPL\n- **Positive catalyst:** Strong Q3 beat (+16% revenue, +29% EPS) supports a constructive near-term view.\n- **Risk:** One-time tariff-refund benefit inflates margins/EPS; mean-reversion risk if guidance implies normalization.\n- **Valuation context:** Dividend yield remains modest; growth is the driver.\n\n## Key Points Summary\n\n| Item | Detail | Implication |\n|------|--------|-------------|\n| Q3 FY26 Revenue | $109.4B, +16% YoY | Strong beat; bullish |\n| Gross Margin | 50.1% (incl. ~2pt tariff refund) | Flattered; ~48% underlying |\n| Diluted EPS | $2.02, +29% YoY (incl. $0.11 benefit) | Strong but partly one-time |\n| Dividend | $0.27/share | Stable income component |\n| Tariff-refund benefit | Non-recurring | Watch Q4 guidance for normalization |\n| Global macro data | Unavailable in corpus | Cannot verify rate/recession outlook |\n| Prediction markets | Unavailable | No market-implied probabilities |\n\n**Bottom line:** AAPL delivered a strong beat with a clear one-time tariff tailwind. Near-term bias constructive, but traders should discount the non-recurring benefit and await guidance on tariff sustainability.\n\n**Recommendation**: Hold\n\n**Rationale**: This debate is genuinely balanced, and that's why I'm landing on Hold rather than committing to either side. The bull makes a legitimate point: even stripping out the one-time tariff refund, AAPL grew revenue 16% YoY to $109.4B with EPS around $1.91 and gross margins near 48%. That's real organic acceleration, and the stock pulling back to $308.91 after a 23% run is not inherently a thesis-breaker. Support at $300 held on record volume, and RSI at 45 is neutral, not broken. The bear, however, raises equally valid concerns: the tariff refund is a reminder of structural cost risk, not just a one-time bonus; record volume on an 8.6% down day (132.5M shares, 2.5x normal) is more consistent with institutional distribution than profit-taking; and the bull has no macro evidence\u2014no CPI, no Fed path, no consumer confidence data\u2014to support the \"resilient consumer\" assumption underpinning the 16% organic growth narrative. The bear is also right that price is below every short-term indicator that matters (10 EMA at $327 is 6% above price), and RSI at 45 means there's room to fall before oversold conditions force buyers in. Neither side has decisively won. The bull's fundamental case is real but partially dependent on a non-recurring benefit and an unverified macro backdrop. The bear's technical case is compelling but lacks confirmation that $300 will actually break. With the stock sitting right at the VWMA and just above the lower Bollinger band, the setup is genuinely two-sided. I can't justify adding exposure into this uncertainty, nor can I justify trimming a company growing 16% organically with fortress-like cash flow. Hold is the honest call.\n\n**Strategic Actions**: Maintain current AAPL position at current sizing. Do not add or trim. Set a hard stop-loss just below $300 (e.g., $295) to cap downside if the bear's distribution thesis plays out and support breaks. If $300 holds and price reclaims the 10 EMA at $327 on above-average volume, that would be the trigger to upgrade to Overweight and add incrementally. If $300 breaks on continued heavy volume, reduce position by 25-50% rather than holding through a potential slide toward $290. Monitor for macro data (CPI, Fed commentary, consumer confidence) as the deciding input\u2014if macro data confirms resilient consumer spending, the bull's organic growth case strengthens and supports upgrading; if macro data shows deterioration, the bear's thesis gains weight and supports trimming. Reassess at next earnings or on a decisive technical break in either direction.\n\n**Action**: Hold\n\n**Reasoning**: The technical report and research plan both converge on a Hold. The stock broke down 8.6% on record volume (132.5M shares, 2.5x normal) after a 23% run, closing at $308.91 \u2014 below the 10 EMA ($327.24) and Bollinger middle ($324.37), with a bearish MACD crossover forming. However, price is sitting just above the lower Bollinger band ($304.22) and at the VWMA ($308.54), with $300 support holding on the day's low, and RSI at 45 is neutral (not oversold, but not broken). The bull's organic 16% revenue growth case is real but partly dependent on a non-recurring tariff refund and an unverified macro backdrop; the bear's distribution thesis is compelling but lacks confirmation that $300 will break. This is genuinely two-sided, so I maintain current sizing with a hard stop just below $300 to cap downside, and I'll upgrade only on a reclaim of the 10 EMA at $327 on above-average volume.\n\n**Entry Price**: 308.91\n\n**Stop Loss**: 295.0\n\n**Position Sizing**: Maintain current position at current sizing; do not add or trim. If $300 breaks on heavy volume, reduce 25-50%.\n\nFINAL TRANSACTION PROPOSAL: **HOLD**\n\n**Rating**: Hold\n\n**Executive Summary**: Maintain current AAPL position at current sizing with a hard stop at $295. Do not add or trim. Tighten the reduction trigger to a close below $304 (lower Bollinger band) on above-average volume, which would prompt a 25-50% reduction. Upgrade to Overweight only on a reclaim of the 10 EMA at $327 on above-average volume. Time horizon: reassess at next earnings or on a decisive technical break in either direction.\n\n**Investment Thesis**: The debate is genuinely two-sided, and neither the aggressive nor the conservative analyst has decisively won. The bull case rests on real organic fundamentals: even stripping out the one-time tariff refund, AAPL grew revenue 16% YoY to $109.4B with ~48% gross margins and $1.91 EPS\u2014objectively strong for a mega-cap. The stock sits at $308.91, essentially on the VWMA ($308.54) and just above the lower Bollinger band ($304.22), with $300 support holding on the day's low and RSI at 45 (neutral, not broken). However, the bear case deserves equal weight: 132.5M shares traded (2.5x normal) on an 8.6% down day is more consistent with institutional distribution than profit-taking, price is below the 10 EMA ($327.24) and Bollinger middle ($324.37), a bearish MACD crossover is forming, and there is no macro data (CPI, Fed path, consumer confidence) to validate the \"resilient consumer\" assumption underpinning the organic growth narrative. The conservative's call to trim 25-50% now is premature\u2014it locks in a loss at $308.91 while price sits at the VWMA and just above the lower band, without evidence support has failed. The neutral analyst's refinement is the correct synthesis: hold current sizing, keep the $295 stop below the $300 support and June consolidation zone ($290-295), but tighten the reduction trigger to a close below $304 on above-average volume rather than waiting for $300 to break. This preserves upside if the bull is right while capping downside faster if distribution confirms. The reclaim of $327 on strong volume remains the upgrade trigger; a break of $304 on heavy volume triggers the 25-50% reduction.\n\n**Price Target**: 327.0\n\n**Time Horizon**: Next earnings or decisive technical break\n\n**Rating**: Hold\n\n**Executive Summary**: Maintain current AAPL position at current sizing with a hard stop at $295. Do not add or trim. Tighten the reduction trigger to a close below $304 (lower Bollinger band) on above-average volume, which would prompt a 25-50% reduction. Upgrade to Overweight only on a reclaim of the 10 EMA at $327 on above-average volume. Time horizon: reassess at next earnings or on a decisive technical break in either direction.\n\n**Investment Thesis**: The debate is genuinely two-sided, and neither the aggressive nor the conservative analyst has decisively won. The bull case rests on real organic fundamentals: even stripping out the one-time tariff refund, AAPL grew revenue 16% YoY to $109.4B with ~48% gross margins and $1.91 EPS\u2014objectively strong for a mega-cap. The stock sits